#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121136 :tutorial/content "Recall that if the curve $\\mathbf r(s)$ is parametrized by arc length $s,$ then the curvature of $\\mathbf{r}$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\kappa(s) =  \\left\\| \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf T}{\\textrm{d}s}  \\right\\|.\n$$\n\nMoreover, if $\\mathbf r(t)$ is parametrized by a general parameter $t,$ we have the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf T(t) = \\dfrac{\\mathbf r'(t)}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|}$ is the unit tangent vector to the curve. \n\nIt can be shown that the curvature of $\\mathbf r(t)$ can also be found using the following cross product formula:\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\|\\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t)\\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^3}\n$$\n\nWe'll derive this formula at the end of the lesson. For now, let's get some practice at applying it.\n\nFor example, suppose we would like to find the curvature of \n\\[\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\left\\langle t, \\: 1, \\: t^2 \\right\\rangle\n\\]\nat the point where [math]t = 0.[/math] \n\nFirst, we calculate [math]\\mathbf r'(t),[/math] [math]\\mathbf r'(0),[/math] and [math]\\|\\mathbf r'(0)\\|\\mathbin{:}[/math]\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf r'(t) &= \\left\\langle \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}t}(t), \\: \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}t}\\left(1\\right), \\: \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}t}\\left(t^2\\right) \\right\\rangle \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\left \\langle 1, \\: 0, \\: 2t \\right \\rangle \\\\[7pt]\n\n\\mathbf r'(0) &= \\langle 1, 0, 0\\rangle \\\\[7pt]\n\n\\| \\mathbf r'(0) \\| & = \\sqrt{1^2 + 0^2 + 0^2} \\\\[3pt]\n&= 1\n\\end{align*}\n\nNow, we find [math]\\mathbf r''(t)[/math] and [math]\\mathbf r''(0)\\mathbin{:}[/math]\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf r''(t) & =  \\left\\langle \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}t}(1), \\: \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}t}\\left(0\\right)  \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}t}(2t) \\right\\rangle \\\\[5pt]\n&=\\langle 0, 0, 2 \\rangle \\\\[7pt]\n\n\\mathbf r''(0) &= \\langle 0,0,2 \\rangle\n\\end{align*}\n\nNext, we compute the cross product and its magnitude:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf r'(0) \\times \\mathbf r''(0)  & = \n\\begin{vmatrix}\n  \\mathbf i&\t\\mathbf j&\t\\mathbf k\\\\\n  1& \t\t0&\t \t0\\\\\n  0& \t\t0& \t\t2\n\\end{vmatrix}\\\\[3pt]\n&= \\begin{array}{|cc|} \n\t\t0 & 0 \\\\\n\t\t0 & 2\n\t\\end{array} \\, \\mathbf{i} - \n\\begin{array}{|cc|} \n\t\t1 & 0 \\\\\n\t\t0 & 2\n\t\\end{array} \\, \\mathbf{j} +\n\\begin{array}{|cc|} \n\t\t1 & 0 \\\\\n\t\t0 & 0\n\t\\end{array} \\, \\mathbf{k} \\\\[3pt]\n&= -2\\,\\mathbf j \\\\[7pt]\n\n\\| \\mathbf r'(0) \\times \\mathbf r''(0) \\| &= 2\n\\end{align*}\n\nTherefore, the curvature at [math]t=0[/math] is\n\n\\begin{align}\n\\kappa(0) &= \\dfrac{\\| \\mathbf r'(0) \\times \\mathbf r''(0) \\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(0)\\|^3} = \\dfrac{2}{1^3} = 2.\n\\end{align}" :tutorial/id #uuid "699ec19c-b03f-5cc8-8096-613245aa4961" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7883 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121137 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a plane curve $C$ that is parametrized by the vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\langle x(t), \\: y(t) \\rangle.\n$$\n\nAt first glance, it looks as though the cross product curvature formula cannot be applied since cross products apply to three-dimensional vectors, whereas the given vector $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ has only two dimensions.\n\n\\[\n\\kappa(t)=\\dfrac{\\|{\\mathbf{r}^{\\prime}(t)}\\times {\\mathbf{r}^{\\prime\\prime}(t)}\\|}{\\|{\\mathbf{r}^{\\prime}(t)}\\|^3}\n\\]\n\nHowever, this curve can also be viewed as a curve in three-dimensional space that lies on the plane $z=0.$ In this case, $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ can be considered as a vector-valued function with $z$-component equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)= \\langle x(t), \\: y(t), \\: 0 \\rangle\n$$\n\nAs a result, the curvature $\\kappa$ of our plane curve $C$ can be calculated using our cross product formula:\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t)=\\dfrac{\\|{\\mathbf{r}^{\\prime}(t)}\\times {\\mathbf{r}^{\\prime\\prime}(t)}\\|}{\\|{\\mathbf{r}^{\\prime}(t)}\\|^3}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2ae1ba4c-311d-5298-8526-146a426bda14" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7884 :tutorial/title "Applying the Cross Product Formula to Plane Curves"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121138 :tutorial/content "Recall that if the curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ is parametrized by a general parameter $t,$ then the curvature of $\\mathbf{r}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf T(t) = \\dfrac{\\mathbf r'(t)}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|}$ is the unit tangent vector to the curve. \n\nHere, $\\mathbf r'(t)$ is a vector function and $\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|$ is a scalar function.\nSo, we differentiate $\\mathbf T(t)$ by using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{∂}{∂t}(T) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂t}(\\frac{{r}^{'}(t)}{‖{r}^{'}(t)‖}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂t}(\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{'}(t)‖}\\cdot {r}^{'}(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂t}(\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{'}(t)‖})\\cdot {r}^{'}(t)+\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{'}(t)‖}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}({r}^{'}(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂t}(\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{'}(t)‖})\\cdot {r}^{'}(t)+\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{'}(t)‖}\\cdot {r}^{''}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the cross product of a vector with itself equals $\\mathbf{0},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t)\\times {T}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{'}(t)}{‖{r}^{'}(t)‖}\\times (\\frac{∂}{∂t}(\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{'}(t)‖})\\cdot {r}^{'}(t)+\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{'}(t)‖}\\cdot {r}^{''}(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{'}(t){‖}^{2}}\\,({r}^{'}(t)\\times {r}^{''}(t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the magnitude of both sides and use the fact that $\\mathbf T$ is a unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖T(t)\\times {T}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{'}(t){‖}^{2}}\\,‖{r}^{'}(t)\\times {r}^{''}(t)‖ \\\\ ‖T(t)‖\\cdot ‖{T}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{'}(t){‖}^{2}}\\,‖{r}^{'}(t)\\times {r}^{''}(t)‖ \\\\ 1\\cdot ‖{T}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{'}(t){‖}^{2}}\\,‖{r}^{'}(t)\\times {r}^{''}(t)‖ \\\\ ‖{T}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\frac{‖{r}^{'}(t)\\times {r}^{''}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{'}(t){‖}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|} = \\dfrac{\\left\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\right\\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^3}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f59936dc-ef8e-53d7-be8e-244d577dd658" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7885 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Cross Product Formula"}]}
